      "Organisations rarely suffer from a bad logo. They suffer from a logo that exists alone. The department that prints the annual report, the officer who builds the presentation, the engineer who labels the site sign and the agency running the campaign are all improvising from the same single file — and the brand fragments quietly.",
      "## What a brand system actually contains",
      "A working identity is a small, boring, complete set: logo variants for every real situation including small sizes and single colour, a defined colour palette with print and screen values, an Arabic and a Latin typeface that visually agree, spacing rules, photography direction, and — most importantly — ready templates for the documents your organisation genuinely produces every week.",
      "If your guidelines document is fifty pages and there is no editable letterhead or presentation template, the guidelines will be ignored within a month. Not out of disrespect: people have deadlines.",
      "## Arabic and Latin must be designed together",
      "Pairing a Latin typeface with whatever Arabic font happens to be installed is the most common identity failure in the region. The two scripts have different vertical rhythms, different weight distribution and different natural line heights. When they are chosen and tuned together, bilingual material looks deliberate. When they are not, every dual-language page looks slightly broken and nobody can say why.",
      "## Design for the worst case, not the presentation slide",
      "Your logo will be embroidered on a cap, engraved on a small metal pen, printed in one colour on a delivery note, stitched onto a flag seen from forty metres and stamped as a rubber seal. If it only works in a large full-colour rendering on a dark background, it is not finished. We test identities at production sizes and in single colour before approval, because production is where identities break.",
      "## Consistency beats sophistication",
      "An organisation that applies a simple system correctly everywhere reads as more established than one with an elegant identity applied inconsistently. Recognition is built by repetition. That is why the deliverable that matters most is not the logo presentation — it is the set of locked templates that make the correct choice the easiest choice for a busy employee.",
      "## Rebranding: change the system, then roll out in order",
      "A rebrand is an operations project. Sequence it: primary digital surfaces, stationery and forms, signage, vehicles, uniforms, stamps and seals, then promotional stock as existing inventory is consumed. Publish the switch date internally, keep a small archive of the old marks for legal and historical documents, and expect the process to take a full year in any organisation with physical assets. Planned properly it looks like confidence; done piecemeal it looks like drift.",
