      "A visitor forms an impression of an organisation from a handful of physical objects: the flag at the entrance, the stamp on their document, the shirt worn by the technician who arrived at their site. These items are rarely in the brand manual, they are usually ordered under time pressure, and they quietly carry more credibility than a campaign.",
      "## Flags: fabric and finishing decide the lifespan",
      "Two flags can look identical on delivery and differ by a year of service life. For outdoor use the fabric weight and the UV behaviour of the dye matter more than the printing method, and the hem is where cheap flags fail first — reinforced double stitching with correctly spaced eyelets is the difference between one season and three. Colour also shifts on fabric: a solid brand colour printed on textile reads slightly softer than the same value on coated paper, so approve a fabric sample rather than a paper proof.",
      "For indoor and desk flags, the base and pole finish carry the impression. A crisp flag on a wobbly plastic base undermines itself.",
      "## Stamps: sharpness, and the layout nobody checks",
      "An official stamp is a legal signal, and a blurred impression makes a document feel unofficial. Laser-engraved plates hold fine detail — small Arabic text, thin logo strokes, registration numbers — where cast rubber closes up. Before production, check the layout at actual size: bilingual stamps in particular tend to be designed at 400 percent on screen and become illegible at 40 millimetres. Also decide the ink colour deliberately; some institutions require a specific colour for particular documents.",
      "Self-inking mechanisms are worth the higher unit price for anything used daily — thousands of consistent impressions, no separate pad drying out on a shelf, and replaceable plates when details change.",
